    id: "b8a6f8c3f1a72419547a",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Separatist party projected to win Quebec election, adding new test to Canada's unity",
    summary: "The Parti Québécois, which secured a minority, has vowed to hold an independence referendum in the years to come.",
    preview: "Canada's Parti Québécois (PQ) is projected to have won enough seats in Quebec's provincial election to form a minority government, though the separatists will need other parties' support to achieve their aims - such as a new public vote on independence. It marks the first time the PQ has been in power since 2014, with Paul St-Pierre Plamondon set to become the province's new leader. In his victory speech, St-Pierre Plamondon again raised his independence plans but vowed to be \"the premier of everyone in Quebec\".",
    body: "Canada's Parti Québécois (PQ) is projected to have won enough seats in Quebec's provincial election to form a minority government, though the separatists will need other parties' support to achieve their aims - such as a new public vote on independence.\n\nIt marks the first time the PQ has been in power since 2014, with Paul St-Pierre Plamondon set to become the province's new leader.\n\nIn his victory speech, St-Pierre Plamondon again raised his independence plans but vowed to be \"the premier of everyone in Quebec\".\n\nThe result could prove a fresh test for Prime Minister Mark Carney, also setting up confrontations with Québec over issues like immigration and the monarchy.\n\nIn its effort to achieve independence, the PQ has pledged to hold a referendum on separating from the rest of Canada.\n\nThe majority French-speaking province has held other votes on this, in 1980 and 1995. On both occasions, the independence movement was defeated. Polls indicate only about 30% of Quebecers back such a move this time.\n\nSt-Pierre Plamondon has previously said the vote would occur after President Donald Trump is out of office south of the border in the United States, explaining that would require \"conditions that allow for an informed and serene debate\". The US and Canada are in a trade war as a result of Trump's programme of tariffs.\n\nIn his victory speech after Monday's election results, St-Pierre Plamondon said of the potential referendum: \"Our national destiny will be achieved one day.\"\n\nCarney told reporters on Tuesday that he spoke with Quebec's new premier-designate and congratulated him on his win.\n\nAsked about the prospect of a referendum, the prime minister said he is focused on current challenges facing Canada, not those \"a few years down the road\".\n\nThe PQ leader will need to secure the support of rival parties to get the vote he wants, potentially from Québec Solidaire, a left-leaning sovereigntist party that won nine seats.\n\nQuebec Liberal Leader Charles Milliard said his party, which won 40 seats, is prepared to support the PQ on a number of issues, but \"there is no way we will collaborate on a potential referendum\".\n\nDespite winning the most amount of seats of any party, the PQ's share of the popular vote is only 28%.\n\n- Canada's Carney stresses unity in the face of challenges at home\n\n- Published22 January\n\nWith most votes counted on Tuesday morning, the party was set to win fewer than the 64 seats needed to command a majority in the province's assembly, according to projections from Canadian media.\n\nThe Liberals - the provincial party with links to Carney's federal Liberals - are projected to have increased their number of seats in the assembly, as have the Conservatives.\n\nAll parties benefited from the collapse of the Coalition Avenir Québec (CAQ), which was previously the province's governing party but is not projected to have won a single seat this time.\n\nCarney may now find himself managing dual unity crises in different parts of the country, depending on the outcome of a vote in Alberta on whether to have a referendum of its own.\n\nTrump is due to leave office in just over two years, meaning that if Plamondon is able to enact his pledge, a third vote on independence would likely be scheduled for 2029 or 2030.\n\nBattle looms over British monarchy\n\nSeparate from the issue of independence, Plamondon has made it clear he is willing to fight Ottawa if it is seen as overstepping matters of provincial interest.\n\nHe suggested during the campaign that he was going to pressure Ottawa, external on issues like immigration, vowing to fight any increases mandated by the Carney government.\n\nHe also pledged to \"abolish the monarchy in Quebec\" and reroute money spent on its institutions to social services instead.\n\n\"We are going to do what we have to do to get the monarchy out of Quebec, repatriate those funds, and send them to social causes,\" he told reporters in late September.\n\nFor example, Plamondon suggested defunding Quebec's lieutenant-governor's office - responsible for representing King Charles III in the province - saying he would instead spend that money to tackle homelessness.\n\nPlamondon was among the Quebec lawmakers who refused to pledge allegiance to the King in 2022, paving the way for a provincial law that now makes the oath of allegiance optional.\n\nHis plan to withdraw financial support for the lieutenant-governor's office is likely to face significant legal challenges, given the monarchy's central role in Canada's constitution.\n\nSuch a move would also put future Quebec laws in doubt, as one of the key roles of the lieutenant-governor is to give royal assent to new legislation.\n\nPressed about these hurdles, Plamondon told reporters that he was ready for a legal battle with the federal government.\n\nMonday's result is in part due to public fatigue with the previous CAQ government.\n\nThe party made the issue of safeguarding Quebec's identity and language central to its eight years in office, but its long-time leader, François Legault, eventually lost significant support among voters.\n\nEfforts to revive the party by replacing Legault with a new leader, outgoing Premier Christine Fréchette, were unsuccessful.\n\nFréchette conceded her own re-election race in Trois-Rivières shortly before 22:00 local time. She said in a speech that she would work with St-Pierre Plamondon on the transition.\n\n\"Governing Quebec is the greatest honour I have ever had,\" she said. \"I have Quebec tattooed on my heart.\"\n\nThe Quebec Liberal Party secured the second-most seats, while the Quebec Conservative Party gained seats.\n\nPrime Minister Carney remains popular in Quebec, though support for him had recently dipped, external as US President Donald Trump imposed new tariffs on Canada in August, following a breakdown in trade talks.\n\nQuebec is among the hardest hit by the latest US tariffs, with experts at global forecasting firm Oxford Economics projecting a C$2bn blow for the province.",
